<section class="lp-section" id="keep-salesforce"><div class="lp-wrap">
{{ g.heading('03','Decide before moving','When Salesforce makes sense.<br>When we would choose something simpler.','A recognised name is a poor reason to take on a system your team does not need.') }}
<div class="dg-decision"><div><h3>Keep or choose Salesforce when…</h3><ul><li>Different teams need detailed access rules and connected processes.</li><li>Custom workflows and integrations support work that matters to the business.</li><li>Someone has the time and skills to maintain the setup.</li><li>The cost of rebuilding a useful system outweighs the savings from switching.</li></ul></div><div><h3>Compare simpler options when…</h3><ul><li>The daily job is finding a lead, making a call, following up and moving a deal.</li><li>Reps avoid the CRM because entering and finding information takes too much effort.</li><li>You keep paying for features nobody can explain or demonstrate.</li><li>Basic changes regularly need outside help.</li></ul></div></div>
<div class="lp-note"><strong>CallTeam verdict: make Salesforce justify the work it creates.</strong><p>If you mainly need a pipeline and reliable follow-up, start with a simpler CRM. Keep Salesforce when its depth solves problems you actually have.</p></div>
<p class="dg-related">Keeping Salesforce and changing only the calling setup? Compare <a href="/resources/orum-alternatives/">Orum alternatives</a> before taking on a CRM migration.</p>
</div></section>
